TRACE_DROP_NODATE_DET	Observation date not determinable for: %1(coverage by the block not verifiable, rated as unblocked)
TRACE_DROP_HUNT_RED	Requests to dropper transport paths (nsgclient packages): dropper search or source IP with an injection line, not a client:
TRACE_DROP_HUNT_DET	Dropper search, not a client: %1(more than two different package names from one address or command characters in the query string). To do: block the addresses, add them to the custom file as a field finding, report to SOC.
TRACE_DROP_YEL	Requests to dropper transport paths (nsgclient packages) from %1 source IP(s) in the window - legitimate Linux clients or dropper pickup, assess:
TRACE_WHEN_DET	When:          %1 to %2
TRACE_BLOCKED_NODATE_FRAG	, no date
TRACE_DROP_ADDR_DET	Address:       %1   %2 request(s)   Dropper search: %3   Block list: %4   Rules file: %5   Address list: dropper_requester
TRACE_NETS_DET	Networks:      %1
TRACE_DROP_RESULT_HUNT_DET	Result:        dropper search (more than two different package names from one address or command characters in the query string). No client does this. No effect on a fixed build, the addresses keep searching.
TRACE_DROP_RESULT_OPEN_DET	Result:        open. Real Linux clients request nsgclient.deb, attackers fetch nsg64.deb and similar packages. The paths below decide.
TRACE_DROP_TODO_BLOCK_DET	To do:         %1. Block the addresses with dropper search in the firewall and add them to ns_quickcheck.blocked_ips. This finding then becomes ORANGE.
TRACE_DROP_TODO_FIELD_DET	               %1. Add as a field finding to the custom file (README 15, Howto Feldbefund) and report to SOC.
TRACE_DROP_TODO_CHECK_DET	To do:         %1. Check the paths below per address: nsgclient.deb from known clients = normal, other package names = block and report.
TRACE_DROP_TODO_PATHS_DET	               %1. Paths per address:
TRACE_DTLS_PPE_RED	DTLS handshake errors AND packet engine restart in the window - possible CVE-2026-88772 attempt, check /var/core
TRACE_NO_ERRLOG	Web server error log not checked: no httperror* file in the window (%1h)
TRACE_ERRLOG_RULES_MISSING	Web server error log not checked: rules HTTPERR_EXEC_RE/HTTPERR_PROBE_RE missing in the rules file (%1; needs %2 or newer)
TRACE_HTTPERR_EXEC_RED	Web server executed or looked for scripts outside the stock paths (%1 interpreter error line(s)):
TRACE_HTTPERR_EXEC_RESULT_DET	Result:        PHP or CGI ran outside the management UI. This only happens if a file is there that does not belong there. Block 5 and runbook.
TRACE_HTTPERR_EXEC_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Review the named files: do they still exist? Content, timestamp, hash on VirusTotal (hash only). 2. Match the time against block 4. 3. If in doubt: incident process, do not patch.
TRACE_HTTPERR_EXEC_NONE_OK	Web server error log without interpreter errors outside the stock paths
TRACE_PHP_ANOM_VULN_YEL	PHP memory/fatal patterns in the management portal on a vulnerable build (CTX697263): %1 line(s), %2 to %3
TRACE_PHP_ANOM_FIXED	PHP memory/fatal patterns in the management portal: %1 line(s), %2 to %3. Build fixed against CTX697263, normal on large configs
TRACE_PHP_ANOM_RESULT_DET	Result:        hypothesis, no published signature. CVE-2026-109618/109619 use out-of-memory and fatal error paths in PHP. The same messages also occur with large configs in the GUI. Only together with block 5 (new PHP file, interpreter children) does this become a finding.
TRACE_PHP_ANOM_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Update to the fix build (block 1). 2. Until then, restrict port 80/443 of the management addresses by ACL (block 2). 3. Read block 5.
TRACE_PHP_ANOM_NONE_OK	No PHP memory/fatal patterns in the management portal above the threshold (%1)
TRACE_PHP_ANOM_RULE_MISSING	PHP anomalies in the management portal not checked: rule HTTPERR_PHP_ANOMALY_RE missing in the rules file (%1; needs %2 or newer)
TRACE_WEBSHELL_200_RED	Webshell path answered with status 200 (the file exists and was called):
TRACE_WEBSHELL_200_TODO_DET	To do:         incident process, do not patch, do not restart. Back up the file (path, hash), read block 5.
TRACE_PROBE	Webshell probing: %1 request(s) to %2 non-existent webshell paths (PROBE_LEVEL=info)
TRACE_PROBE_YEL	Webshell probing: %1 request(s) to %2 webshell path(s), the files do not exist (attempt without effect):
TRACE_PROBE_NOTE_DET	Note:          [client 127.0.0.2] in the error log is the appliance itself. The packet engine passes requests internally to the web server, which never sees the real address. Who it was is in the access log at the same second with the same path; the script looked there.
TRACE_PROBE_SRC_DET	Source:        %1, %2 request(s)%3
TRACE_PROBE_NO_SRC_DET	%1 request(s) without source (access log rotated or path differs in the access log)
TRACE_PROBE_SRC_UNKNOWN_DET	Source:        unknown. The paths are not in the access log of the window (rotated) or the request came through the management layer
TRACE_PROBE_RESULT_DET	Result:        someone is checking whether this appliance carries the known webshells (.ctxs.receiver, .local_journal, PHP variants). The answer was 404; block 5 confirms that the files are absent.
TRACE_PROBE_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Block the source addresses and add them to ns_quickcheck.blocked_ips. 2. If block 5 does not report the paths, nothing else to do. PROBE_LEVEL=info in the config downgrades this message to INFO.
TRACE_PROBE_NONE_OK	No webshell probing in the web server error log
TRACE_WEBSERVER_DIRECT_YEL	Web server addressed directly: error lines with a client outside 127.0.0.x:
TRACE_WEBSERVER_DIRECT_RESULT_DET	Result:        someone reached Apache without going through the packet engine, i.e. via the NSIP (management) or a path that bypasses the gateway logic.
TRACE_WEBSERVER_DIRECT_TODO_DET	To do:         assess the address (admin network? then set MGMT_NET_RE in ns_quickcheck.conf), otherwise check management access on SNIPs and block the address.
TRACE_NO_MGMTLOG	Management access not checked: no httpaccess.log file in the window (%1h)
TRACE_MGMT_RULE_MISSING	Management access not checked: rule MGMT_PATH_RE missing in the rules file (%1; needs %2 or newer)
TRACE_MGMT_ADDR_FRAG	%1 (%2 request(s))
TRACE_MGMT_EXTERNAL_YEL	Management layer (NITRO, GUI) addressed from addresses outside RFC 1918 and MGMT_NET_RE:
TRACE_MGMT_EXTERNAL_RESULT_DET	Result:        NITRO or GUI are reachable from outside or were addressed from outside. This is the staging path for CVE-2026-88771 (block 2: management access on SNIP).
TRACE_MGMT_EXTERNAL_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Are these your admin networks? Then set MGMT_NET_RE in ns_quickcheck.conf. 2. Otherwise: disable management access on SNIPs (set ns ip <snip> -mgmtAccess DISABLED), block and record the addresses.
TRACE_MGMT_INTERNAL_OK	Management layer (NITRO, GUI) addressed from internal addresses only
TRACE_NO_AUTHLOG	Logons not checked: no auth.log file in the window (%1h)
TRACE_AUTH_RULES_MISSING	Logons not checked: rules AUTH_LOGIN_RE/AUTH_FAIL_RE missing in the rules file (%1; needs %2 or newer)
TRACE_SSH_FAIL_FRAG	%1 (%2 failed attempts)
TRACE_SSH_USER_FROM_FRAG	%1 from %2
TRACE_SSH_BACKDOOR_RED	Logon of a known backdoor account on the appliance: %1
TRACE_SSH_EXTERNAL_YEL	SSH logons from addresses outside RFC 1918 and MGMT_NET_RE:
TRACE_SSH_EXTERNAL_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Are these your admin workstations or jump servers? Then set MGMT_NET_RE. 2. Otherwise change the account password, check sessions, block and record the address.
TRACE_SSH_BRUTEFORCE_YEL	SSH failed attempts in series (brute force) from:
TRACE_SSH_BRUTEFORCE_TODO_DET	To do:         allow SSH on the NSIP only from admin networks (ACL or firewall). Put the addresses on the block list.
TRACE_SSH_INTERNAL_ONLY	Logons on the appliance in the window: %1internal addresses only
TRACE_AUTH_NONE_OK	No logons or failed attempts in auth.log in the window
# --- AAA: Block 4 Gateway-Anmeldefehler
AAA_RULES_MISSING	Gateway logon failures not checked: rules AAA_FAIL_RE/AAA_OK_RE missing in the rules file (%1; needs %2 or newer)
AAA_OWN_HAPEER	HA peer
AAA_BF_HIT_RED	Successful logon after brute force on an account (%1 account(s)): password found or user logged on after the attack, a human must decide:
AAA_BF_HIT_ROW	Account %1  %2 failed attempts from %3 address(es), then successful from %4
AAA_BF_HIT_TODO_DET	To do:         lock the account, reset the password, end sessions (kill aaa session -userName <account>), call the user: was it them? Block and record the addresses.
AAA_BF_YEL	Brute force on single accounts (many passwords against one known account, %1 account(s)):
AAA_BF_ROW	Account %1 %2 failed attempts  %3 address(es): %4  %5 to %6, up to %7/min  Success after: no
AAA_BF_MORE_ROW	and %1 more accounts
AAA_BF_RESULT_DET	Result:        the attacker knows the account name and tries passwords. Unlike spraying, the attack targets one person. Clarify today: does the account exist, is it locked, does it have MFA, where is the name known from (LinkedIn, leak, phishing).
AAA_BF_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Lock the account or change the password, end sessions. 2. Block and record the addresses. 3. Check lockout on the appliance (README 15, Howto Lockout). Caution: lockout lets anyone lock known accounts with failed attempts.
AAA_HIT_RED	Successful logon after a failure series from an attack address (%1 address(es)): the campaign found a password:
AAA_HIT_ROW	%1  %2 failed attempts, %3 accounts, then successful: %4
AAA_HIT_TODO_DET	To do:         lock the account now, reset the password, end sessions (kill aaa session -userName <account>), check MFA. Block and record the address.
AAA_SLEEPER_YEL	Sleeper (slow spraying over days, below all thresholds, %1 address(es)):
AAA_SLEEPER_ROW	%1 %2 failed attempts %3 accounts   on %4 day(s) over %5 days (%6 to %7), at most %8/min   Success after: no  [Note: accounts %9]
AAA_SLEEPER_RESULT_DET	Result:        deliberately slow so lockout and rate thresholds do not trigger. Visible only on the time axis (window and cumulative address list).
AAA_SLEEPER_TODO_DET	To do:         block and record the address. It will come back, the block list will show it.
AAA_SPRAY_YEL	Password spraying (many accounts, few attempts per account): %1 failed attempts in the window, %2 address(es) above the thresholds, %3 of them machines:
AAA_SPRAY_SPLIT_DET	%1 address(es) unclear (human pace without success, listed first), %2 machines (by failed attempts), %3 users (success after, no finding):
AAA_RATE_MACHINE	Rating: attack, block
AAA_RATE_MACHINE_NET	Rating: attack (network with machines), block
AAA_RATE_UNCLEAR	Rating: unclear, monitor
AAA_RATE_UNCLEAR_BURST	Rating: unclear, one account in a short burst: typically a client with an old saved password, call the user
AAA_RATE_USER	Rating: user (success after)
AAA_SPRAY_ROW	%1 %2 failed attempts %3 accounts  %4 to %5, up to %6/min  Success after: %7  %8  [Note: accounts %9]
AAA_SPRAY_MORE_DET	and %1 more address(es), complete in the address list of this run
AAA_TOP_ACCOUNTS_DET	Accounts with the most failed attempts: %1
AAA_TOP_ACCOUNT_SUCCESS_FRAG	, success after
AAA_ACCOUNTS_TOTAL_DET	%1 different accounts in total, %2 of them only once (dictionary or leak spraying)
AAA_SPRAY_RESULT_DET	Result:        machine = %1 or more attempts/min or %2 or more accounts from one address, or an address in a /24 with at least two machines; user = success of the same account within %3 min; unclear = human pace without success (also behind carrier NAT); one account in a short burst = usually a client with an old password. No CVE relation, but a risk for accounts without MFA.
AAA_SPRAY_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Block and record networks and machine addresses at the firewall (more effective than lockout: spraying with one attempt per account stays below any lockout threshold). 2. Check MFA for all gateway accounts. 3. Tune the thresholds (AAA_FAIL_IP, AAA_RATE, AAA_FAIL_USERS, AAA_OK_WIN, AAA_BF_MIN) in ns_quickcheck.conf.
AAA_BELOW_THRESHOLD	Gateway logon failures in the window: %1, no address above the thresholds (%2 failed attempts, %3 accounts)
AAA_OWN_HIT_RED	Successful logon after a failure series from an own address (%1): an internal host tries accounts and succeeded (lateral movement or service account):
AAA_OWN_YEL	Logon failures from own or internal addresses (%1 address(es)):
AAA_OWN_INTERNAL_FRAG	 (internal)
AAA_OWN_ROW	%1%2   %3 failed attempts   %4 account(s)   %5 to %6, up to %7/min   Success after: %8   [Note: accounts %9]
AAA_OWN_RESULT_DET	Result:        not the external attacker, but needs clarification: service account or monitoring with an old password, admin test, proxy or NAT egress with users behind it, or an internal host probing the gateway as a target (lateral movement). Own addresses are not in the address lists.
AAA_OWN_TODO_DET	To do:         1. Name the system behind the address (the ns.conf object is in the line). 2. Check the account: who owns it, why does it fail. 3. If it recurs over days and is not a service account, treat it as an incident.
AAA_NONE_OK	No gateway logon failures in the window
