TRUST_NOTE_FRAG	 [Note: %1]
TRUST_TAG_FRAG	 [address in trusted_ips%1]%2
TRUST_EVT_INTERNAL	rated as internal (management, SSH or logon failures)
TRUST_LISTED_BEFORE_YEL	%1 is in trusted_ips but was listed as %2 in this appliance's address list - check the entry (typo? intended?)
TRUST_ACTIVE_TRU	Trusted address %1%2 active in the window [Note: %3]:
TRUST_NOTE_NONE	none
TRUST_UNTIL_PAREN_FRAG	 (valid until %1)
TRUST_UNLIMITED_PAREN_FRAG	 (valid without expiry)
TRUST_EXPIRED_TAG_FRAG	 [in trusted_ips, expired on %1: renew or delete the entry]
TRUST_EXPIRED_LIST_FRAG	, trusted expired on %1
TRUST_CHECK_DET	Check:         is the entry correct and still wanted? Wrong entry = delete the line from ns_quickcheck.trusted_ips, then the next run rates it again. Impact findings (injection, webshell 200, success after failure series, active connection) stay red for trusted addresses too.
# --- CHECK: Block 5 Kompromittierungs-Schnellcheck
CHECK_HOSTFILE_NOT_ELF_YEL	%1 exists (not a binary) - check
CHECK_PAYLOAD_PATH_RED	Known payload path exists: /%1 (%2)%3
CHECK_WEBSHELL_NAMES_RED	Webshell file names in web directories:
CHECK_PHP_OUTSIDE_RED	PHP files outside admin_ui/ns_gui (no PHP belongs there):
CHECK_LAST_UPGRADE	Last upgrade according to nsinstall.log: %1
CHECK_PIN_MISMATCH_YEL	%1 hash-approved web file(s) changed again (whitelist pin no longer matches): check the content, then update the pin
CHECK_WEB_BURST	%1 web files changed in one burst on %2 (rewrite by upgrade aftermath or theme deploy, within %3 s) - not a single finding
CHECK_WEB_MANY_YEL	%1 web files changed since boot, spread over %2 min - spot-check:
CHECK_WEB_FEW_YEL	%1 web file(s) changed since boot (outside the upgrade window) - check each one:
CHECK_WEB_DIR_GROUP_ROW	%1/: %2 files, %3 to %4
CHECK_WEB_WHEN_DET	When:          %1 to %2, last boot %3
CHECK_PHP2SHELL_RED	New PHP file(s) plus PHP memory/fatal patterns on a vulnerable build (CTX697263): pattern of a PHP2Shell exploitation
CHECK_PHP2SHELL_TODO_DET	To do:         incident process, do not patch, do not restart. Back up the files (path, hash), isolate the management addresses by ACL.
CHECK_WEB_RESULT_PHP_DET	Result:        atypical: %1 PHP file(s) changed. That is not a theme.
CHECK_WEB_RESULT_OUTSIDE_DET	Result:        %1 file(s) outside themes, images and favicons. Assess one by one.
CHECK_WEB_RESULT_EXEC_DET	Result:        %1 executable file(s) among them (scripts, plugins.xml): not a theme image, a theme ticket does not explain this by itself.
CHECK_WEB_RESULT_THEME_DET	Result:        all changes in theme folders, images or favicons. A theme change by the operator is the likely explanation.
CHECK_WEB_CODE_DET	Code:          executable code. Diff against the previous version (snapshot set or backup) before approval. Approve only with a hash pin, lines created by: sh ns_quickcheck.sh --pin <directory>
CHECK_WEB_TODO1_DET	To do:         1. Check the change date against the theme change ticket.
CHECK_WEB_TODO2_DET	               2. Add confirmed files to ns_quickcheck.whitelist as webfile:<regex>$, for code with @sha256=<hash>. The message disappears (README 15, Howto Whitelist).
CHECK_WEB_TODO3_DET	               3. Unexplained files: review the content, look up the hash on VirusTotal (hash only), if in doubt incident process.
CHECK_WEB_NONE_OK	No web files changed since boot
CHECK_HTTPD_CHANGED_RED	httpd.conf changed %1, not at boot time, and content with a finding:
CHECK_HTTPD_CONTENT_RED	httpd.conf: PHP in gateway paths or foreign alias:
CHECK_HTTPD_CHANGED_YEL	httpd.conf changed %1, not at boot time. Content without finding (aliases, PHP handlers)
CHECK_HTTPD_WHAT_DET	What happened: the web server configuration has a timestamp that does not match the last boot. Its content was checked and is clean; the message is about the timestamp only.
CHECK_HTTPD_HARMLESS_DET	Harmless if: an install or upgrade happened at that time (installns writes the file). Otherwise: compare against /nsconfig/httpd.conf and a stock version.
CHECK_CRON_ROOT_YEL	Root crontab lines outside the allowed list:
CHECK_CRON_LASTRUN_DET	%1: last run according to /var/log/cron: %2
CHECK_CRON_NOT_IN_WINDOW	not in the window
CHECK_CRON_NOBODY_RED	Crontab for user nobody present (%1 lines) - known persistence
CHECK_LISTENER_EXT_RED	Listeners of interpreter/shell processes on reachable addresses:
CHECK_LISTENER_LO_YEL	Unknown interpreter listeners on loopback (stock services are in KNOWN_LISTENER_RE):
CHECK_MONITOR_STOCK	Monitor script runs under the stock daemon (PID %1, parent: %2) - regular mechanism
CHECK_SUSPECT_PROC_CRON_YEL	Process matches SUSPECT_PROC_RE under cron (PID %1, PPID %2) - not the stock mechanism (daemon monuploadd), assess:
CHECK_SUSPECT_PROC_RED	Process matches SUSPECT_PROC_RE outside the stock daemon (PID %1, parent: %2) - manual start of the monitor script (CISA SIGMA S7):
CHECK_UNKNOWN	unknown
CHECK_MONITOR_CHILD_RED	Child process of the monitor script (PID %1) is a shell or downloader - execution of a log injection:
CHECK_HASH_NO_STOCK	%1: sha256 %2 (no stock value stored for build %3, documentation only)
CHECK_HASH_STOCK_OK	%1: hash matches the stock value for %2
CHECK_HASH_MISMATCH_RED	%1: hash differs from the stock value for %2 (is %3) - file modified
CHECK_OUTBOUND_RED	Active connection to a payload host or known attacker address:
CHECK_LOCAL_USERS_YEL	Local system users besides nsroot: %1 - confirm each one (attackers create their own, e.g. sec_monitor)
CHECK_SEC_MONITOR_RED	System user sec_monitor = known backdoor (LevelBlue)
CHECK_EPA_NOAUTH_RED	EPA default group NO_AUTH in ns.conf - EPA effectively off (Wiz payload)
CHECK_SH_SETUID_RED	/bin/sh has setuid/setgid (%1) - post-exploitation step
CHECK_SVC_USER_CHANGED	Service user of this procedure (%1) was created or changed between the last two saves (%2 line(s), no finding)
CHECK_SAVED_DIFF_RED	%1 saved config change(s) to users, auth, EPA or management between %2 and %3 - confirm each one:
CHECK_SAVED_DIFF_YEL	%1 saved config change(s) to security-relevant objects between %2 and %3:
CHECK_DIFF_ADDED	[new]
CHECK_DIFF_REMOVED	[removed]
CHECK_SAVED_DIFF_NONE_OK	No security-relevant changes between ns.conf.0 and ns.conf
CHECK_UNSAVED_RED	%1 unsaved line(s) in the running config, including user, auth or policy changes:
CHECK_UNSAVED_YEL	%1 unsaved line(s) in the running config (own change without save, or a finding):
CHECK_DIFF_RUNNING_ONLY	[running only]
CHECK_DIFF_SAVED_ONLY	[saved only]
CHECK_UNSAVED_NONE_OK	Running config matches ns.conf (no unsaved changes)
CHECK_RUNNING_EMPTY	show ns runningConfig returned no usable output
CHECK_AAA_SESSIONS	Active AAA sessions: %1 line(s) (details in the report)
CHECK_SIMPLEACL	Simple ACLs present: %1 line(s)
CHECK_CMP_NO_SPACE	--compare %1: not enough space under %2 (%3 KB free, about %4 KB needed) - set %5 not extracted
CHECK_CMP_EXTRACT_FAIL	--compare %1: set %2 could not be extracted (%3)
CHECK_CMP_NO_SET	--compare %1: no snapshot set found in %2/out
CHECK_CMP_AGAINST	Comparison against snapshot %1 (%2)
CHECK_CMP_CHANGED	CHANGED
CHECK_CMP_NEW	NEW
CHECK_CMP_WEB_MANY_YEL	%1 web files new or changed since snapshot %2 (from 20 on usually an upgrade or theme rewrite):
CHECK_CMP_WEB_YEL	%1 web file(s) new or changed since snapshot %2:
CHECK_CMP_WEB_NONE_OK	No web files new or changed since snapshot %1
CHECK_CMP_NEW_USER_RED	System user new since snapshot %1: %2
CHECK_CMP_NEW_LISTENER_YEL	Listener new since snapshot %1:
CHECK_CMP_NO_MANIFEST	--compare: no manifest under %1
CHECK_NO_HITS_OK	No quick check hits (does not replace a full IoC scan)
# --- VERDICT: Block 6 Verdict
VERDICT_FORENSICS_HEAD	Signs of compromise found. Do not reboot, do not upgrade before evidence is secured:
VERDICT_FORENSICS_STEP1	  1. Isolate the appliance (failover to a patched node or firewall), check HA file sync, check the peer as well.
VERDICT_FORENSICS_STEP2	  2. Secure: show techsupport, /var/log, /var/core, the named files, RAM if possible (CTX694799).
VERDICT_FORENSICS_STEP3	  3. Involve Citrix Support, request the official IoC scan. Then rebuild, rotate all secrets including the KEK, revoke certificates.
VERDICT_EXPLOITED_FRAG	, actively exploited
VERDICT_UPDATE_EOL	Release %1 is end of life. No fix; migrate to a supported train.
VERDICT_UPDATE_VULN	Build %1 is vulnerable to %2 (%3%4). Target: %5-%6.
VERDICT_UPDATE_INCOMPLETE	Build %1: assessment incomplete (build not determinable or train not in the rules file). Read block 1.
VERDICT_UPDATE_SAML	SAML configured and a SAML-dependent CVE open (see block 1: %1). Target: %2-%3.
VERDICT_UPDATE_TRACES	Attack traces present: back up logs and /var/core externally before the upgrade, the upgrade overwrites traces.
VERDICT_UPDATE_NO_TRACES	No traces in the window: still back up logs externally before the upgrade (internet-exposed appliance = assume breach).
VERDICT_UPDATE_ISN	After the upgrade: set ns tcpParam -enhancedISNGeneration ENABLED (CVE-2026-88778), in partitions too.
VERDICT_UPDATE_HA	HA: secondary first, failover, then primary. After the upgrade, save ns config and repeat this check.
VERDICT_INV_COVERAGE	Not fully checked:%1 An OK is not possible without this data. Provide logs (increase the --hours window, snapshot set with archive 3) and repeat.
VERDICT_INV_HEAD	The appliance is patched and shows no signs of compromise. There are observations someone must assess; they are listed above with 'To do'.
VERDICT_INV_REPEAT	Then repeat this check: once the points are clarified or whitelisted, the result becomes OK.
VERDICT_INV_OPEN_POINTS	Open points:
VERDICT_INV_ISN	  - Enable Enhanced ISN Generation (the build alone does not close CVE-2026-88778).
VERDICT_INV_NO_MITIG	  - Attack attempts ongoing, no mitigation policy bound: set a responder policy with -type AAA_REQUEST, watch the hit counter.
VERDICT_INV_TRACES	  - Pass the source IPs above to firewall/SOC. Search firewall/DNS logs for the download hosts.
VERDICT_INV_UNSAVED	  - Clarify unsaved config lines: own change, then save ns config; otherwise a finding.
VERDICT_INV_IOC_SCAN	  - Official Citrix IoC scan (Console or Support) as a second opinion if the appliance was exposed before the fix.
VERDICT_OK_COVERED	Build fixed, no signs of compromise. %1 finding(s) covered by blocks (ORANGE above). The next run checks whether the blocks hold.
VERDICT_OK	Build fixed, no traces in the window, no quick check hits. Next check after the next bulletin or when something looks odd.
VERDICT_SAVE_MISSING_NOTE	NOTE: ns.conf is still from build %1. Before the next reboot: save ns config (otherwise changes since the upgrade are lost).
VERDICT_HA_PEER	HA-PEER: check %1 as well: sh %2/%3 --hours %4
# --- ADDR: Adressliste im Report, Beobachtungstexte, Koepfe der Adresslisten
ADDR_SRC_INJ_LINE	ns.log injection line
ADDR_SRC_LOGON_AT_ATTEMPT	httpaccess logon request at attempt time
ADDR_SRC_RESPONDER_LINE	ns.log responder line from IP
ADDR_SRC_CRASH_TRIGGER	request before crash of %1 (120 s window, rough match)
ADDR_SRC_DROPPER	httpaccess DROPPER_PATH_RE
ADDR_SRC_WEBSHELL_200	httpaccess webshell path 200
ADDR_SRC_WEBSHELL_PROBE_TRUSTED	httpaccess webshell path (trusted)
ADDR_SRC_WEBSHELL_PROBE	httpaccess webshell path
ADDR_SRC_HTTPERR_CLIENT	httperror client
ADDR_SRC_MGMT_PATH	httpaccess.log management path
ADDR_SRC_AUTH_ACCEPTED	auth.log Accepted
ADDR_SRC_AUTH_FAILED	auth.log Failed
ADDR_SRC_AAA_FAIL_TRUSTED	ns.log AAA failed attempts (trusted)
ADDR_SRC_AAA_BF_TRUSTED	ns.log AAA brute force on account (trusted)
ADDR_SRC_AAA_BF	ns.log AAA brute force on account
ADDR_SRC_AAA_HIT	ns.log AAA success after failure series
ADDR_SRC_AAA_SLEEPER	ns.log AAA failed attempts over days
ADDR_SRC_AAA_MACHINE	ns.log AAA failure series (machine)
ADDR_RUN_LIST_HEAD	Addresses of this run, added to ns_quickcheck_observed_ips.tsv:
ADDR_STATUS_NEW	new
ADDR_STATUS_KNOWN	known since %1, on %2 day(s)
ADDR_BLOCKED_NO	Block list: no
ADDR_BLOCKED_SINCE	blocked since %1
ADDR_RUN_LIST_TOTAL	  %1 entry/entries. Plain list without trusted addresses: grep -v '^#' ns_quickcheck_observed_ips.tsv | grep -v '^trusted_' | cut -f2 | sort -u
ADDR_IPS_HEAD1	# observed external addresses of this run, summarized per category: kategorie TAB adresse TAB anzahl TAB beobachtung TAB gesperrt (bemerkung|seit from ns_quickcheck.blocked_ips or netscaler ACL; internal, remove before sharing)
ADDR_IPS_HEAD2	# Categories: payload_download_host (named in the injection payload), injection_source (logon request at attempt time, behind NAT the NAT address),
ADDR_IPS_HEAD3	#   dropper_requester (requests to nsgclient paths; legitimate Linux clients possible), outbound_hit (active connection to an attacker address)
ADDR_IPS_HEAD4	# Check before building a block list: shared infrastructure (CDN, Cloudflare WARP, proxies, NAT) blocks bystanders too. No RFC 1918 or own addresses.
ADDR_CUM_HEAD1	# ns_quickcheck_observed_ips.tsv - cumulative address list of this appliance, updated per run. Host %1, since %2, %3 entries, as of %4
ADDR_CUM_HEAD2	# Columns: kategorie TAB adresse TAB anzahl_zuletzt TAB zuletzt_fenster_h TAB anzahl_max TAB max_fenster_h TAB tage TAB zuerst_gesehen TAB zuletzt_gesehen TAB beobachtung TAB gesperrt (bemerkung|seit; internal, remove before sharing)
ADDR_CUM_HEAD3	# anzahl_zuletzt = hits of the last run in its window (zuletzt_fenster_h hours); anzahl_max = highest value ever seen, with its window. Not a sum: every run reads the same logs again.
ADDR_CUM_HEAD4	# tage = calendar days on which a run saw the address (several runs per day count once). Files from script versions before 17.28 (sum, runs) are taken over, their old numbers are too high. For clean values delete the file, it rebuilds itself.
ADDR_CUM_HEAD5	# Categories: payload_download_host, injection_source, dropper_requester, outbound_hit (meaning: README 5.2)
ADDR_CUM_HEAD6	# Check before building a block list: shared infrastructure (CDN, Cloudflare WARP, proxies, NAT) blocks bystanders too. Contains no own and no RFC 1918 address.
ADDR_CUM_HEAD7	# Plain address list without trusted addresses (category trusted_*): grep -v '^#' ns_quickcheck_observed_ips.tsv | grep -v '^trusted_' | cut -f2 | sort -u
ADDR_RUN_SUMMARY	Address list of this run: %1_ips.tsv (%2 entries, %3 of them new against the existing list, %4 address(es) on the block list; cumulated since %5: %6 addresses in ns_quickcheck_observed_ips.tsv)
ADDR_SINCE_TODAY	today
# --- SHARE: Share-Datei
SHARE_HEAD	# ns_quickcheck share %1  as of %2  source ns_quickcheck  (no appliance ID, no notes; only reliable categories; review before sending)
SHARE_FILES	Address lists anon: %1 and %2. Share file for ceterion (after review): %3
# --- FOOT: Fusszeile und Dateihinweise
FOOT_CREATED	Created with netscaler-quickcheck %1 (rules stand %2), Apache 2.0, ceterion AG: ceterion.com/netscaler-quickcheck
FOOT_FILES	Report: %1  JSON: %2 (defanged)  anon: %3 and %4 (for sharing)
